<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  CLion CMake profiles for this ESP-IDF project.

  IMPORTANT: building on the host is NOT supported. All builds run inside the
  ESP-IDF dev container (ESP-IDF v6.1). The top-level CMakeLists.txt enforces
  this by requiring DOCKER_IMAGE=esp32_sensors, which is set by the Dockerfile.

  CLion reaches the container through the Dev Container defined in
  .devcontainer/devcontainer.json, which reuses the `dev` service from
  docker-compose.yml. CMake, Ninja, and the Xtensa toolchain therefore run in
  the container.

  FILE FORMAT
  ===========
  CLion stores these profiles through CMakeSharedSettingsStorage, whose XML
  state is the element *children* of <component name="CMakeSharedSettings">.
  It reads `configurations` -> `configuration` and understands only these
  attributes:
      PROFILE_NAME, ENABLED, CONFIG_NAME, TOOLCHAIN_NAME,
      GENERATION_DIR, BUILD_OPTIONS, GENERATION_OPTIONS,
      GENERATION_PASS_SYSTEM_ENVIRONMENT, ADDITIONAL_GENERATION_ENVIRONMENT,
      NO_GENERATOR, FROM_PRESET

  Do NOT use the pre-2023 shape (`<option name="configurations"><list>` with
  BUILD_DIR / CMAKE_OPTIONS / GENERATOR_NAME): the current loader finds no
  `configurations` child there, so the project ends up with ZERO CMake
  profiles and the CMake run configuration fails with "cannot find any
  profile". Edit the profile in Settings | Build, Execution, Deployment |
  CMake, or update the attributes above - never reintroduce the old names.

  WHAT THIS PROFILE IS
  ====================
  The root CMakeLists.txt is a plain aggregator - `project(... NONE)` - that
  only defines one custom target per subproject (mcgowan_build, test_build,
  espidf-cpp-test_build, donny_build), each shelling out to `idf.py`. So this
  profile gives CLion a working *build* entry point, not a code model:

    * No C/C++ languages are enabled in the root project, so it consumes no
      compiler settings (`-DIDF_TARGET`, `-DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON`
      would only be reported as "Manually-specified variables were not used by
      the project"), and no `compile_commands.json` is produced.
    * Consequently CLion cannot index the firmware here: code navigation and
      C++ refactorings are unavailable through this profile. Use
      `scripts/dev.py build` / the "Build firmware (container)" run
      configuration for real work, or open `mcgowan/` (which is a standalone
      ESP-IDF project) if you want an indexed code model.

  BUILD DIRECTORY
  ===============
  CLion uses its OWN build directory, `cmake-build-esp32s3/`, which it
  generates and owns. This is deliberately separate from the subprojects'
  build trees (`mcgowan/build_esp32s3/` for `idf.py`, driven by
  `scripts/dev.py build`). Sharing a directory does not work: idf.py and
  CLion overwrite each other's CMake cache and Ninja state.

  The profile name "Espressif ESP32-S3" is referenced by the shared CMake run
  configuration (.idea/runConfigurations/esp32_sensors__container_CMake_.xml).
  TOOLCHAIN_NAME is the Dev Container toolchain ("Default", the system toolset
  inside the container); the generator is left unspecified so CLion uses Ninja.
-->
<project version="4">
  <component name="CMakeSharedSettings">
    <configurations>
      <configuration PROFILE_NAME="Espressif ESP32-S3" ENABLED="true" CONFIG_NAME="Debug" TOOLCHAIN_NAME="Default" GENERATION_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$/cmake-build-esp32s3" />
    </configurations>
  </component>
</project>
